Thanks — I can walk through what’s deductible, whether to itemize, how to treat the home office, and what forms you’ll need. I’ll also show an illustrative calculation so you can see likely tax effects. A few quick clarifying questions (below) — answer if you want a tighter calculation.

Assumptions I’ll use in the example below
- You’re married filing jointly for 2023.
- Your amounts are gross amounts for the year: W‑2 wages $79,400; self‑employment (IT contracting) gross $22,100; interest income $1,780 (Discover), $290 (Interactive Brokers).
- The $950 “unreimbursed expenses” are business expenses for the contracting work (not W‑2 employee unreimbursed expenses). If they’re employee expenses, they are not deductible for 2018–2025 under current law.
- You rent your home ($1,720/month) and use a 155 sq ft room exclusively as your home office in a 750 sq ft unit.

If any assumption is wrong (for example the $22,100 is net already, or the $950 are employee expenses), tell me and I’ll adjust.

What you can deduct (federal, 2023)
- Student loan interest: You paid $4,100, but the federal deduction is limited to $2,500 (above-the-line adjustment) and phases out at higher MAGI. With your income level (see estimate below) you should be eligible for the full $2,500 federal deduction.
- Self‑employment business expenses: If your contracting income is self‑employment income, ordinary and necessary business expenses (the $950 and any other documented costs) are deductible on Schedule C.
- Home office (if self‑employed): Allowed only if the space is used regularly and exclusively for the business. Two options:
  - Simplified method: $5.00 × 155 sq ft = $775 (single-line calculation, no Form 8829).
  - Regular method: prorate actual home expenses by business-use percentage. Business percentage = 155 / 750 ≈ 20.67%. Rent $1,720 × 12 = $20,640 annual rent. Business portion ≈ $20,640 × 20.6667% ≈ $4,267 (you can also include a portion of utilities, renter’s insurance, etc.). Use Form 8829 to calculate and report the regular-method deduction. In your case the regular method (≈$4,267) gives a much larger deduction than the simplified method ($775).
  - Note: home office deduction reduces your Schedule C net profit; it cannot create an excess loss beyond other limits (it’s limited to business income from that activity).
- Self‑employment tax: You must pay self‑employment (SE) tax on net self‑employment earnings (Schedule SE). You can deduct half of the SE tax as an above-the-line deduction on Form 1040.
- Charitable contributions: Your $800 cash donation to Northwest Harvest is deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). Keep the receipt.
- Interest income: Report the $1,780 and $290 on your 1040 (1099‑INT / 1099 statements).
- W‑2 income: Report as usual.

What you cannot deduct (under current law, unless special circumstances apply)
- Unreimbursed employee business expenses (for W‑2 employees) — suspended for 2018–2025.
- Student loan interest over the $2,500 limit for federal purposes.
- Personal portion of rent, utilities, etc. (only the business share via home office is deductible for self‑employed).

Itemize vs. standard deduction
- Standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly in 2023 = $27,700.
- Typical itemizable items you have: $800 charitable + state and local tax (SALT) up to $10,000 maximum (this would include state income tax withheld on your W‑2, any property tax if you own property, etc.). As a renter you likely don’t have mortgage interest or property tax to add.
- Even with the SALT max ($10,000) + $800 charity = $10,800 — well below $27,700. So you will almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction unless you have additional itemizable deductions (large medical expenses over the 7.5% AGI threshold, big unreimbursed casualty losses, or significant other deductible items).
- Conclusion: Take the standard deduction unless you can list > $27,700 in itemizable deductions.

Illustrative federal calculation (estimate) — based on assumptions above
1. Total gross income:
   - W‑2 wages: $79,400
   - Contracting (gross): $22,100
   - Interest (Discover + IB): $1,780 + $290 = $2,070
   - Total = $103,570

2. Business deductions (assumed):
   - Contracting expenses: $950
   - Home office (regular method): ≈ $4,267
   - Net self‑employment income = 22,100 − 950 − 4,267 = ≈ $16,883

3. Self‑employment tax (approx):
   - Net earnings for SE tax = 16,883 × 0.9235 ≈ $15,590
   - SE tax ≈ $15,590 × 15.3% ≈ $2,384
   - Half SE tax deductible (above-the-line) ≈ $1,192

4. Above-the-line adjustments:
   - Student loan interest deduction (limited) = $2,500 (max)
   - Half SE tax ≈ $1,192
   - Total above-the-line adjustments ≈ $3,692

5. Adjusted Gross Income (AGI) estimate:
   - 103,570 − 3,692 ≈ $99,878

6. Standard deduction (MFJ) = $27,700 (likely better than itemizing)
7. Taxable income estimate:
   - ≈ $99,878 − 27,700 = $72,178

8. Resulting federal income tax and total self-employment tax:
   - You’d still owe the SE tax (~$2,384) in addition to the income tax computed on taxable income. I didn’t compute the exact federal tax liability here because tax brackets, credits, withholding, and other items affect the final number — but this shows the major items and how the home office reduces SE income and AGI.

Forms and documents you’ll need (federal)
- Form 1040 — main tax return.
- Schedule 1 (Form 1040) — for adjustments to income (student loan interest, deduction for half of SE tax, etc.).
- Schedule C (Form 1040) — report self‑employment income and business expenses for your IT contracting.
- Schedule SE — compute self‑employment tax.
- Form 8829 — if you use the regular method to compute the home office deduction (do NOT use 8829 if using simplified method).
- Form 1099‑INT (from Discover) and any 1099s from Interactive Brokers (1099‑INT, 1099‑DIV, 1099‑B) — report interest/dividends/capital gains appropriately.
- 1099‑NEC / 1099‑MISC if clients issued you one(s) for contracting income (report that income on Schedule C).
- Form 1098‑E — student loan interest statement (shows interest paid). Keep this to substantiate the student loan interest deduction.
- Receipts and records: $800 charitable receipt; records supporting the $950 business expenses; documentation of home rent and utilities and how you measured the home office (floor plans, square footage calculation).
- State return (Illinois IL‑1040) — report income to Illinois. Note: state treatment of some deductions differs; check state rules (student loan interest is generally a federal deduction — state rules vary).

Other practical points / tips
- If your contracting is self‑employment, you should be making estimated quarterly tax payments for income and SE tax; if you haven’t, be prepared for either a balance due or possible penalties.
- Keep clear documentation that the home office is used exclusively and regularly for business (photos, floor-plan, dates, percentage calculation, bills showing rent and utilities).
- If the $950 is actually W‑2 unreimbursed employee expenses, they are not deductible for federal tax years 2018–2025 — don’t claim them on Schedule A.
- If you prefer simplicity and don’t want to prepare Form 8829, the simplified home office method ($5/sq ft) is allowed up to 300 sq ft — but in your case the regular method yields a larger deduction.
- Consider talking to a tax preparer/CPA if you want help preparing Schedule C/8829 and estimating quarterly payments or if you have additional deductions or credits (child tax credit, education credits, etc.).

