Thanks — useful detail. Below I’ll:

- summarize what’s deductible from what you listed,
- show whether you should itemize or take the standard deduction,
- explain how to treat the home office (two methods, with numbers), and
- list the forms you’ll need and other practical steps / questions.

Important: this is general guidance, not formal tax advice. If your situation is unusual (a spouse with separate income, capital gains, etc.) or you want exact tax numbers, consider a tax preparer or CPA.

1) Quick summary of your situation (used for calculations below)
- Filing status: Married filing jointly (MFJ)
- W-2 wages (Riverside Hospital): $79,400
- Self‑employment / contracting income: $22,100 (2 clients)
- Interest from Discover savings: $1,780 (likely Form 1099‑INT)
- Investment income from Interactive Brokers: $290 (please confirm whether this is interest, dividends, or capital gains)
- Rent: $1,720/month for a 750 sq ft home → annual rent = $20,640
- Home office: 155 sq ft exclusive use (155 / 750 = 20.667%)
- Student loan interest paid: $4,100 (FedLoan) — loan from 2018–2019
- Charitable cash gift: $800 (have receipt)
- Unreimbursed expenses: $950 — please confirm whether these are business expenses for your contracting work (if yes, deduct on Schedule C). If they are unreimbursed employee expenses from someone else’s employer, they are not deductible for 2018–2025.

2) What you can (likely) deduct from the items you listed
- Student loan interest: Up to $2,500 may be deductible as an above‑the‑line adjustment on Form 1040 if your Modified AGI is below the phaseout range for MFJ (for 2023 the phaseout for MFJ begins at $145,000 and fully phases out at $175,000). Based on the income you reported, you should qualify for the full $2,500 (you paid $4,100, but the limit is $2,500).
- Charitable contributions: $800 is deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). If you take the standard deduction, you don’t get a separate deduction for that gift.
- Unreimbursed expenses: If these $950 are business expenses related to your contracting (tools, software, supplies, travel for your IT clients), they are deductible on Schedule C. If they are unreimbursed employee expenses (you were an employee and your employer didn’t reimburse), they are not deductible for tax years 2018–2025.
- Home office: Deductible only if you’re self‑employed and the space is used regularly and exclusively for business. You said it’s exclusive 155 sq ft — that meets the exclusivity test. Two methods:
  - Simplified method: $5 × 155 sq ft = $775 deduction (easy).
  - Regular method: allocate business percentage (20.6667%) of actual home expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, etc.). From rent alone: $20,640 × 20.6667% ≈ $4,267.20. If you have utility and other allocable expenses, the deduction could be larger. Regular method requires Form 8829.
- Self‑employment tax: Your contracting income is subject to self‑employment tax (Social Security + Medicare). You’ll compute that on Schedule SE; you are allowed an above‑the‑line deduction on Form 1040 equal to half of your self‑employment tax.

3) Standard deduction vs. itemize
- 2023 standard deduction for MFJ = $27,700.
- Likely itemizable amounts you reported: charitable $800, state/local taxes (SALT) up to $10,000 if you paid state income tax or property tax, mortgage interest (none reported), etc. Based on the items you listed, you very likely will not beat the $27,700 standard deduction just with $800 charity and typical renter situation.
- Recommendation: take the standard deduction. Note: the home office deduction is not tied to Schedule A; it reduces business net income on Schedule C and therefore reduces your AGI even if you take the standard deduction.

4) Home office: which method to use (numbers)
I’ll show approximate effects using only the data you gave (no utilities listed). I’ll also assume the $950 are business expenses. Two scenarios:

A) Simplified method
- Home office deduction: $775
- Business deductions: $950 (other business expenses) + $775 = $1,725
- Self‑employment net profit: 22,100 − 1,725 = 20,375
- Net earnings subject to SE tax = 92.35% × 20,375 ≈ 18,818
- SE tax ≈ 15.3% × 18,818 ≈ $2,878
- Deductible half SE tax ≈ $1,439 (an adjustment on Form 1040)
- Student loan interest adjustment (max) = $2,500
- This reduces your AGI but note regular method below likely gives a larger deduction.

B) Regular method (rent only used here)
- Home office deduction from rent alone: $20,640 × 20.6667% ≈ $4,267
- Business deductions: $950 + $4,267 = $5,217
- Self‑employment net profit: 22,100 − 5,217 = 16,883
- Net earnings subject to SE tax = 92.35% × 16,883 ≈ 15,588
- SE tax ≈ 15.3% × 15,588 ≈ $2,384
- Deductible half SE tax ≈ $1,192
- This yields a noticeably lower AGI than the simplified method because the home office share of rent is substantial. If you have other allocable expenses (utilities, renter’s insurance), regular method would increase the deduction further.
- Recommendation: Given your rent and 20.7% business share, the regular method (Form 8829) will likely produce a bigger deduction than the simplified method. Use regular method if you can document rent, utilities, etc.

5) Forms you’ll need
- Federal:
  - Form 1040 (main return)
  - Schedule 1 (Adjustments to income) — for student loan interest deduction and adjustment for half SE tax
  - Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report contracting income and business expenses
  - Schedule SE — to compute self‑employment tax
  - Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you choose the regular method for home office deduction (not needed for simplified method)
  - Form 1099‑NEC / 1099‑MISC: you may receive these from clients (report income even if you didn’t receive a 1099)
  - Form 1099‑INT (from Discover) and Form 1099‑DIV / 1099‑B (from Interactive Brokers) as applicable — report the amounts even if you don’t get forms
- Illinois:
  - IL‑1040 (state income tax return) — Illinois taxes taxable income at the state rate (file separate state return)
- Estimated taxes (if you owe self‑employment tax and income tax not covered by W‑2 withholding): Form 1040‑ES for quarterly payments (for future years or if you need to make estimated payments to avoid penalties).

6) Other practical points and records to keep
- Keep receipts and bank statements for the $800 charitable donation and any business expenses, and your student loan interest statements (Form 1098‑E or account statements).
- Keep a log or floor plan showing the exclusive business use of the 155 sq ft space and how you computed the percentage. For the regular method, keep records of rent, utilities, insurance, repairs allocated to the home office.
- If you didn’t make estimated tax payments for 2023 and you will owe more than you had withheld, you may be subject to an underpayment penalty. You can estimate and pay the balance when filing or pay electronically now.
- If the $290 from Interactive Brokers was a sale of securities with capital gains, you may need Form 8949 and Schedule D. If it’s interest or dividends, it will go on 1040 with Form 1099‑INT or 1099‑DIV.

7) Questions I have for you (so I can refine calculations)
- Is the $950 in unreimbursed expenses for your contracting business? If not, what are those expenses?
- Is the $290 from Interactive Brokers interest, dividends, or capital gains?
- Does your spouse have income or deductions to include? (If yes, that affects AGI and student loan interest phaseout.)
- Do you have utility / insurance / repair amounts for the home that could be included in the regular-method home office calculation?
- Did any client issue 1099‑NEC? Did you receive Form 1099‑INT / 1099‑DIV / 1099‑B?

8) Next steps I can help with
- If you want, I can: (a) prepare a more precise worksheet and estimated tax calculation using any additional numbers you provide, or (b) draft an email summarizing documents to give to a tax preparer, or (c) create a checklist of records to gather before filing.

If you confirm the answers to the questions above (especially whether the $950 is business expense, nature of the $290, spouse income), I’ll run the more precise numbers and show a likely federal tax and self‑employment tax bill and whether any estimated‑tax penalty might apply.

