Thanks — this is enough to give a clear roadmap. I’ll summarize what you can/don’t can deduct, whether itemizing will help, how to treat the home office, the forms you’ll need, and next steps/questions so I can give more precise dollar estimates.

Quick summary
- Most likely you will take the 2023 standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly ($27,700) unless you have additional itemizable deductions (mortgage interest, large state/local taxes up to the $10k SALT cap, large medical expenses, or more charitable contributions) that push your Schedule A total above $27,700.
- Your self-employment (contracting) income must be reported on Schedule C and will be subject to income tax and self-employment tax (Schedule SE). Many of the expenses you listed (home office, unreimbursed expenses if they are business expenses) are deductible on Schedule C.
- Student loan interest (up to $2,500) is an “above-the-line” adjustment to income on Schedule 1 if you qualify (it phases out at higher MAGI).
- Interest from Discover and Interactive Brokers is taxable and will generally be reported on Schedule B (combined interest > $1,500).

Details and calculations (with the numbers you gave)

1) Income you must report
- W-2 from Riverside Hospital: $79,400 (Form W-2 → Form 1040)
- Contracting (self-employment) income: $22,100 (Schedule C)
- Interest: $1,780 (Discover) + $290 (Interactive Brokers) = $2,070 (report on Form 1040; because total interest > $1,500 use Schedule B)

2) Home office (you said 155 sq ft exclusive; total home 750 sq ft; rent $1,720/month)
- You can claim a home office deduction only if the space is used regularly and exclusively for your self-employment (contracting) work. It is NOT available for W-2 employment.
- Two methods:
  a) Simplified method: $5/sq ft up to 300 sq ft → 155 × $5 = $775 (easy, no Form 8829).
  b) Actual-expense method: allocate a share of actual home expenses by square footage:
     - Business % = 155 / 750 = 0.20667 (≈ 20.67%).
     - Annual rent = $1,720 × 12 = $20,640.
     - Rent portion allocable to business ≈ 20.67% × $20,640 ≈ $4,271.
     - If you also have utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs, etc., the business share of those can also be deducted.
     - For sole proprietors using the actual method you normally complete Form 8829 (Business Use of Your Home) and attach it to Schedule C.
- Usually the actual method will give a larger deduction for your numbers (rent allocation alone is ~ $4,270 vs. $775 simplified). Use actual method if you have records for rent/utilities/insurance/repairs and you have sufficient net profit to absorb the expense.

3) Unreimbursed expenses ($950)
- If these are unreimbursed employee expenses from your W-2 job: currently (under the tax law applicable for 2018–2025) miscellaneous itemized employee unreimbursed expenses are NOT deductible — they are suspended.
- If these $950 are ordinary and necessary expenses of your contracting work (supplies, phone, software, travel for clients, etc.), they ARE deductible on Schedule C and reduce self-employment income.

4) Student loan interest ($4,100 to FedLoan)
- The maximum student loan interest deduction is up to $2,500 per year (an “above-the-line” deduction on Schedule 1), subject to MAGI phase-outs. If you qualify you can deduct up to $2,500; the excess ($4,100 − allowed amount) is not deductible.
- Whether you qualify (full amount, partial, or none) depends on your Modified Adjusted Gross Income (MAGI). I can estimate your MAGI and exactly how much of the $4,100 is deductible if you want — I’ll need to know any other adjustments (IRA/SEP contributions, HSA, half of SE tax, etc.).

5) Charitable donation ($800 to Northwest Harvest)
- Cash donations to qualified charities are deductible on Schedule A (itemized). You should keep the receipt (you said you have it).
- Because the standard deduction for MFJ in 2023 is $27,700, $800 of charitable contributions alone won’t make itemizing beneficial unless you have other itemizable items (mortgage interest, significant state/local taxes up to $10k SALT cap, big medical expenses, etc.).

6) Impact on whether to itemize vs take standard deduction
- Standard deduction 2023 (MFJ) = $27,700.
- Your known itemized item currently = $800 charitable. Unless you have additional itemizable deductions (mortgage interest, property tax, state income tax withheld > ~$26k? — note SALT cap is $10k), you will almost certainly be better taking the standard deduction.
- Home office and business expenses reduce your self-employment income on Schedule C — they are NOT part of Schedule A itemized deductions and therefore do not influence the standard-vs-itemize decision.

7) Self-employment tax, estimated payments, and deductible portion
- Self-employment income will be subject to self-employment tax (Social Security + Medicare) computed on Schedule SE. Half of the self-employment tax is deductible above-the-line (reported on Schedule 1).
- If too little tax is withheld from your W-2 and no estimated payments are made for the contracting income, you may owe estimated taxes (Form 1040-ES) and possibly penalties. Consider increasing W-2 withholding or making quarterly estimated payments.

8) Forms you’ll likely need to file (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return)
