What you can deduct (federal)
- Self‑employment (contracting) business expenses: You may deduct ordinary and necessary expenses of the contracting work on Schedule C. That includes things like supplies, phone/Internet (business portion), and business-related unreimbursed expenses — if the $950 you listed is for your contracting work, it’s deductible on Schedule C. If the $950 are unreimbursed employee expenses related to your W‑2 job, those are NOT deductible for 2018–2025 under current law.
- Home office: Deductible only for the self‑employment activity (not for W‑2 employment). Two methods:
  - Simplified method: $5.00/sq ft × 155 sq ft = $775 for the year.
  - Actual-expense method: allocate 20.67% of qualifying home expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs, etc.) to the business. Based only on rent: $1,720/mo × 12 = $20,640 × 20.67% ≈ $4,270. (If you have utilities/insurance/repairs add their business portions too.) If you use the actual method you generally file Form 8829 with Schedule C. Use whichever method gives the larger deduction (actual here looks bigger).
  - Note: home office deduction cannot exceed your net self‑employment income from that business; excess may carry forward as a loss subject to limits.
- Self‑employment tax deduction: you pay self‑employment tax (Social Security + Medicare) on net SE income; one-half of that SE tax is an above‑the‑line deduction on Schedule 1 (reduces AGI).
- Student loan interest: up to $2,500 is deductible as an adjustment to income (Schedule 1), subject to MAGI phaseouts. Your MAGI (~$103k) is well below the 2023 phaseout for married filing jointly ($145k–$175k), so you can claim the full $2,500 even though you actually paid $4,100.
- Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction: you may be eligible for up to 20% of your qualified business income from the contracting activity (Form 8995 or 8995-A). Because your total taxable income is well below the thresholds for 2023, QBI is likely available (amount depends on your net Schedule C profit after expenses).
- Interest income reporting: interest from Discover ($1,780) + Interactive Brokers ($290) = $2,070. Because total interest > $1,500, you will generally need to complete Schedule B (Interest and Ordinary Dividends).

What you cannot deduct (or are limited)
- Charitable contribution: $800 to Northwest Harvest is deductible only if you itemize on Schedule A. The 2023 standard deduction for married filing jointly is $27,700, so unless you have other sizable itemizable amounts (state & local taxes up to $10,000 cap, mortgage interest, large medical expenses, etc.) itemizing is unlikely beneficial. If you take the standard deduction, the $800 charitable gift is not deductible on your 2023 federal return.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses: not deductible for W‑2 work (suspended through 2025) — only deductible if they are business expenses for your self‑employment.
- Student loan interest limit: capped at $2,500 (not the $4,100 you paid).

Should you itemize or take the standard deduction?
- Likely take the standard deduction ($27,700 for MFJ in 2023). Based on the items you listed, your itemizable deductions probably won’t exceed $27,700 (you listed only $800 charity; you didn’t list mortgage interest or large state/local taxes beyond the $10k SALT cap).
- Important: taking the standard deduction does NOT prevent you from deducting self‑employment business expenses (including home office) — those are reported on Schedule C regardless.

Forms you’ll likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 — main return.
- Schedule 1 (Form 1040) — adjustments to income (student loan interest deduction and half of SE tax deduction appear here).
- Schedule B — if interest income (total > $1,500) or certain foreign accounts. Your interest total $2,070 → use Schedule B.
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss From Business) — report the contracting income $22,100 and business expenses (home office, business supplies, $950 if a business expense).
- Schedule SE — calculate self‑employment tax; half of this tax is deductible (Schedule 1).
- Form 8829 — if you use the actual expense method for business use of your home. If you use simplified method, you don’t use Form 8829 — you just enter the simplified amount on Schedule C.
- Form 8995 (or 8995-A) — for the Qualified Business Income deduction (QBI), depending on complexity/amount.
- Schedule A — only if you choose to itemize (to claim the $800 charitable donation and any other itemizable items).
- Illinois state return: IL-1040 (Illinois has its own return and tax). Illinois does not allow municipal (Chicago) income tax (there is none), but you still must file IL-1040. State deductions/credits differ from federal, so check state rules or ask a preparer.

